WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:05.720
In 2008, the Visceral Games title Dead Space
introduced us to Isaac Clarke, a lowly engineer

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:08.580
forced to fight his way off a zombie-infested
spaceship.

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:10.200
It was an instant hit.

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:14.700
Dead Space racked up over two million sales,
nabbed a handful of awards, and spawned sequels

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:17.340
in 2011 and 2013.

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:19.090
And then…nothing.

00:00:19.090 --> 00:00:22.449
Here's why we never got to play Dead Space
4.

00:00:22.449 --> 00:00:24.670
Dead Space 3 didn't sell

00:00:24.670 --> 00:00:29.429
In an interview with CVG, former Electronic
Arts president Frank Gibeau said,

00:00:29.429 --> 00:00:33.640
"Ultimately you need to get to audience sizes
of around five million to really continue

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:36.070
to invest in an IP like Dead Space."

00:00:36.070 --> 00:00:39.530
Sadly, Dead Space 3 didn't sell anywhere close
to five million copies.

00:00:39.530 --> 00:00:43.750
In its first week of release, Dead Space 2
shipped nearly two million copies.

00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:48.219
By contrast, Dead Space 3 moved only 605,000
units.

00:00:48.219 --> 00:00:53.170
That was well below EA's initial estimates,
and game's failure contributed to EA's year-on-year

00:00:53.170 --> 00:01:00.470
revenue loss of over $150 million in 2013,
likely making it difficult to justify a sequel.

00:01:00.470 --> 00:01:02.010
Critics were critical

00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:06.770
According to the review aggregator Metacritic,
the first two Dead Space games received average

00:01:06.770 --> 00:01:11.750
review scores of 89% and 90% respectively
for the Xbox 360 versions.

00:01:11.750 --> 00:01:14.320
Dead Space 3, on the other hand, only scored
a 78%.

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:18.040
While this is still slightly above average
for video games, it was a disappointment for

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:22.640
the franchise, with critics knocking the title's
move away from survival horror towards more

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:23.790
generic action.

00:01:23.790 --> 00:01:28.560
Mediocre reviews and tepid word of mouth from
players may help explain why sales died an

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:30.080
unspeakably brutal death.

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:39.179
Survival horror is changing

00:01:39.579 --> 00:01:43.880
Despite some innovative features, the first
two Dead Space games are very much traditional

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:45.540
survival horror titles.

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:49.909
Most of Dead Space's tension comes from navigating
the game's harsh and violent environments

00:01:49.909 --> 00:01:54.360
with limited resources, while some strategically
placed gore and a smattering of haunted-house

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:56.200
style jump scares round everything out.

00:01:58.080 --> 00:01:59.460
(Monster screams)

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:02.540
But a lot has changed since the first Dead
Space came out.

00:02:02.540 --> 00:02:08.160
In 2014, a "playable trailer" called P.T.
took the gaming world by storm with its haunting,

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:10.500
surreal imagery and shifting geography.

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:15.410
The game P.T. promoted, a Silent Hill reboot
by Hideo Kojima and acclaimed film director

00:02:15.410 --> 00:02:20.250
Guillermo del Toro, never materialized, but
the trailer was enough to galvanize the survival

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:21.580
horror genre.

00:02:21.580 --> 00:02:26.390
With games moving towards psychological horror,
Dead Space's action formula was beginning

00:02:26.390 --> 00:02:28.920
to look, well, dated.

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:30.770
The stars have moved on

00:02:30.770 --> 00:02:35.730
Glen Schofield, who created Dead Space, left
Visceral and Electronic Arts in 2009 to form

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:37.770
his own company, Sledgehammer Games.

00:02:37.770 --> 00:02:42.360
Wright Bagwell, lead gameplay designer on
Dead Space and creative director on Dead Space

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:46.920
2, joined Zynga in 2011, where he led the
design team on FarmVille 2.

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:51.120
And Steve Papoutsis, Visceral's vice president
and general manager and the lead executive

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:55.070
on all of the Dead Space games, departed the
company in 2015.

00:02:55.070 --> 00:03:00.170
In other words, the people who made the franchise
great in the first place have left.

00:03:00.170 --> 00:03:01.700
Star Wars

00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:06.480
Inspired by Dead Space's popularity, Electronic
Arts and DICE handed Visceral the reigns for

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:08.260
the shoot-em-up Battlefield Hardline.

00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:12.920
And from there, Visceral's main office went
straight to work on an ambitious and mysterious

00:03:12.920 --> 00:03:16.590
Star Wars title, overseen by Uncharted's Amy
Hennig.

00:03:16.590 --> 00:03:20.760
That's exciting news for fans of both Star
Wars and Visceral, but it doesn't leave much

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:21.960
time for Dead Space.

00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:26.040
Visceral is booked for the next few years,
and if there's going to be more Dead Space,

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:30.450
it'll have to wait until Visceral returns
from its trip to a galaxy far, far way.

00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:32.720
Or, farther away than their usual one.

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:37.170
"And I hate to break it to you losers, but
Han Solo's a bitch."

00:03:37.170 --> 00:03:38.810
It lost its way

00:03:38.810 --> 00:03:42.880
The first two Dead Space games are conscious
throwbacks to movies like Alien, with the

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:46.790
schlubby Isaac forced to fight his way alone
through infested, confined spaces..

00:03:46.790 --> 00:03:48.480
But Dead Space 3 is different.

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:53.130
In a conscious attempt to reach a wider audience,
Electronic Arts added co-op multiplayer to

00:03:53.130 --> 00:03:57.940
the main campaign - and a stereotypically
grizzled soldier buddy named John Carver.

00:03:57.940 --> 00:04:02.900
The tight, claustrophobic levels are replaced
by snow fields and wide-open space, allowing

00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:07.610
a suddenly more nimble Isaac to simply dodge
out of the way of danger, and duck effortlessly

00:04:07.610 --> 00:04:09.110
behind protective cover.

00:04:09.110 --> 00:04:11.680
In other words, the series lost what made
it special.

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:13.890
Getting it back will be no easy feat.

00:04:13.890 --> 00:04:16.040
A reboot makes more sense

00:04:16.040 --> 00:04:19.840
Visceral's Star Wars game is still a number
of years away—and that doesn't count extra

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:24.770
time that the studio will spend on post-launch
downloadable content and a potential sequel.

00:04:24.770 --> 00:04:29.161
By that point, it will have been well over
a half decade since the last Dead Space game

00:04:29.161 --> 00:04:30.280
hit consoles.

00:04:30.280 --> 00:04:34.650
Even with the tacked on cliffhanger ending
from the DLC, it might make more sense for

00:04:34.650 --> 00:04:39.440
the company to reboot the franchise with a
new direction and new characters rather than

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:41.540
continue with something that was no longer
working.

00:04:41.540 --> 00:04:45.660
It could be that Dead Space is officially
dead, but if the series is anything like its

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:49.530
necromorph monsters, it’ll come back to
life before long.

00:04:49.530 --> 00:04:52.590
(more monster screams!)

00:04:52.590 --> 00:04:54.470
On second thought… maybe it shouldn’t.

00:04:54.470 --> 00:04:55.970
Thanks for watching!

